data.content:
The lightest approach for modern .NET (8+) is the built-in **`TimeProvider`** class — no DI container needed, just a constructor parameter:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In tests**, use the built-in fake:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

(`FakeTimeProvider` lives in `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`.)

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- No interface to define — `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+).
- No DI container required — the optional parameter keeps the default behaviour.
- Production callers don't change: `new InvoiceCalculator()` still works.

